<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ninomiya Sontoku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1787">1787</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ninomiya Sontoku was a Japanese agrarian reformer and moral philosopher from Sagami Province. Rising from a ruined farming family, he restored villages for the Ōkubo lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and later for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> through careful accounting, frugality, mutual aid and land reclamation. His teaching, known as hōtoku ('repaying virtue'), inspired a widespread rural improvement movement, and he became a model of diligence in modern Japanese schools.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ninomiya-sontoku.jpg" alt="Ninomiya Sontoku" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tak1701d · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Ninomiya_Sontoku_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
